cmnmm1l1t001lmy01rlh7lipw	cmnmly03000ahmy01q57enr20	Healthy Grocery Shopping reel As you can see, it's always an excursion when we head to the supermarket as a family of 7... But it's also a lot of fun and they all look forward to coming with me because they get to pick their own favourite fruit and veg! Back when I had 3 under (which happened 3x if you’d believe it!), I was super stressed out every time we attempted to do our groceries because it felt like I didn’t have enough hands to do everything plus keep the kids from wrecking the aisles 😅 Thankfully, grocery runs have become a lot easier these days as the older ones are more independent to help organise the carts and carry the baskets! We take our grocery runs very seriously in this family, going 3 to 4 times a week to restock our fresh fruit, veg, and dairy! That's because I think it's really important to involve the children in picking out their own food from a young age, so they know what they consume and grow up with a better understanding of what a healthy diet is. We home-cook all weekday meals and always have a variety fresh fruit washed and cut in the fridge, for easy access to encourage them to eat more fruit. I've also found that the Healthy 365 App also has great resources to help me teach the kids about what a healthy plate should look like, as well as meal tracking functions to help me gauge the calories we consume daily. Added my kids' profiles to the app via Singpass so that I get age-appropriate resources for each of them too! These tools really help me feel more in control over my kids’ daily habits, and I feel more equipped to help them live healthier lives 🥰 Screen-free reel SO very pleased that we managed to do all these in lieu of screens while spending time in and around home! Not including all the scheduled outdoor walks, tennis and basketball games, and cycling we did because I wanted to round up our super low effort screen-free activities which the kids were happy to turn to by themselves - that's a reel for another time. I'll be honest, screen time is a hot button topic in my house, every single day. We have very strict house rules in place when it comes to screens, and the children all know it, but that doesn't stop them from requesting for more time or asking to watch TV or play their games on days they aren't supposed to. I'm not being over-scrupulous, I've seen first-hand the negative effects of execessive screen-time in my kids, and trust me if I could, I'd choose a 100% screen-free life for my family. But, sometimes, I really need the space to work out or clean up or work without one of the younger ones straggling off me or yelling out "MAMA" on repeat, so I decided that allowing screens in small, controlled doses as one available form of entertainment in the house is the best way for us. But other times, I will always remind them of other things they can do besides watching TV or playing games… I have many thoughts on screens, and I'll forever die on this hill that less is better. So I'm very thankful for apps like the Healthy 365 App, which provides age-appropriate resources pertaining to screen use, according to each child I've added - which is particularly useful because each child actually has a different recommended screen limit because of their age and gender. I also particularly like that I can book Active Family Programme activities for my children directly on the app, which is great because more outdoor time instantly means less screens and more sweat! How do you limit your kids’ screen time while they’re at home everyday? I’d love to hear more ideas from you! ☺️	cmeqlonw80000p6011bcdxgfp	creator	2026-04-06 03:08:05.202
